{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3175","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3175","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3175\",\"parent_revision\":\"b74706d90ce11605cb3cb49a16e1b35671c861928fa965c8bfa53734f5acf4d1\",\"root_revision\":\"b74706d90ce11605cb3cb49a16e1b35671c861928fa965c8bfa53734f5acf4d1\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P0\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA `prek` stash cycle removed 22 markdown edits from the working tree during a commit sequence. The commit reported success, `git status` then read clean, and the edits were present in no commit. No message was emitted at any point.\\n\\nObserved 2026-08-23 during a path-scoped commit sequence over a large unstaged set. The work was recovered in full from `~/.cache/prek/patches/1787447822038-87601.patch` and verified by content before being re-committed, so nothing was lost on this occasion. It was noticed only because the author re-read the files afterwards.\\n\\n`AGENTS.md` gotcha 12 documents this mechanism, and documents a different symptom:\\n\\n> prek stashes unstaged changes before running hooks. If a formatter hook (ruff-format, etc.) modifies staged files and the stash cannot restore cleanly, prek rolls back the hook's changes and the commit fails (\\\"Stashed changes conflicted...\\\").\\n\\nThat describes a loud failure: the commit fails and names the reason. What occurred was the quiet variant \u2014 the commit succeeded and the unstaged edits disappeared. An author following the documented gotcha would have no reason to check, because the documented signal never appears.\\n\\nImpact: work is destroyed silently during a normal, sanctioned operation. The conditions appear to involve a path-scoped commit (`git commit <paths>`) run while a large unstaged set exists, which is precisely the pattern this repository's own commit-cadence guidance prescribes \u2014 commit by explicit file list rather than blanket staging, specifically so a concurrent agent's in-progress edits are not swept in. So the guidance that protects against one hazard steers directly into this one.\\n\\nNothing in the repository records that `~/.cache/prek/patches/` holds the stashed content, so an author who does not know to look there has no recovery path and no indication one exists.\\n\\nNot established: the exact trigger conditions, whether the unstaged set size is causal or incidental, whether the behaviour is version-specific to the installed `prek`, and whether the patch file is retained reliably or is subject to cleanup.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: the reporting agent's proposed mitigation was to commit or stage all intended work before running a path-scoped commit over a large unstaged set. Recorded as a hypothesis for grooming to validate, not as a requirement \u2014 it may trade this hazard for the concurrent-agent one the path-scoped guidance exists to prevent.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 22 markdown edits recovered from the prek patch cache, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"1b81652e168d0fa0d5183c6fffb53d4a68cf6445ab8ff6ec5ebdf4086c682d41\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQPWucg\"}"}